speechtext:
1. Initiatives under the Small Farm Development Program (SFDP) were designed to assist small-scale low-income farmers to adjust to changing circumstances, including changing technology and demands for farm products. The primary purpose of this contract was to assess the need for changes in the program in the context of the extent and nature of the small-scale low-income farmer problem.
2. The principal conclusions and recommendations were:
(a) SFDP resources should be directed primarily towards an
estimated 51,000 full-time and 14,000 part-time low-income
farmers;

December 19, 1978
Order Paper Questions
(b)	The program should provide farm mangement services to assist low-income farmers to take a whole-farm approach to the development of their business and to take advantage of their unique objectives, capabilities and resources, within the framework of existing institutions and farm communities;
(c)	SFDP farm management counsellors should concentrate on farmers earning 50 per cent or more of their income from farming with total assets not greater than $150,000;
(d)	SFDP rural development counsellors should concentrate on rural residents with net family incomes below $6,000 and net worths below $50,000.
3. This study was contracted outside the Department of Agriculture because;
(a)	Staff resources were unavailable at the time to carry it out;
(b)	Non-governmental perspectives on policy and program alternatives were desired.
